For semiconductor materials datasets, a minimal scholarly record is not just the pair (material, gap). It is (sample identifier, composition, polytype, structural characterization, temperature, measurement observable, instrument/method, uncertainty, source DOI or dataset version, analysis method and units). Band-offset datasets require two named materials plus interface orientation/termination and the common energy reference. The National Institute of Standards and Technology's ''InterMat'' work illustrates that even computationally predicted offsets require an explicit underlying method, corrected electronic structure and test dataset.<ref name="intermat">K. Choudhary and K. Garrity, [https://www.nist.gov/publications/intermat-accelerating-band-offset-prediction-semiconductor-interfaces-dft-and-deep “InterMat: Accelerating Band Offset Prediction in Semiconductor Interfaces with DFT and Deep Learning”], ''Digital Discovery'' (2024), DOI [https://doi.org/10.1039/D4DD00031E 10.1039/D4DD00031E].</ref>

A literature-mined dataset containing over 100,000 extracted gaps is a useful discovery index but not the same thing as 100,000 independently standardized measurements. Duplicates, composition ambiguity, provenance, text-mining confidence, experimental vs computed values and publication selection have to be audited before quantitative inference.<ref name="bandgap"/>

== Model selection and unresolved engineering limits ==

* '''Equilibrium vs driven operation:''' one Fermi level is appropriate at thermal equilibrium; driven carrier populations may require separate quasi-Fermi levels.
* '''Nondegenerate vs degenerate:''' the Boltzmann formulas and conventional Einstein relation are not universal at high carrier density.
* '''Bulk vs interface:''' trapping, surface recombination, Schottky barriers, band offsets and oxide charge can dominate a device despite good bulk material.
* '''Diffusive vs ballistic:''' drift–diffusion is a continuum model; short transport lengths, high electric fields and quantum confinement can require nonlocal or quantum transport.
* '''Electrical vs system merit:''' faster or smaller individual devices do not guarantee a faster or more efficient system without interconnect, memory, thermal, yield, power-delivery, software and workload considerations.
* '''Verification vs explanation:''' a citation supports a particular scientific premise or historical account; experimental device performance still requires actual measurements under stated conditions.

